            "decimals": 1,
            "label": "Effective bandwidth",
            "note": "The highest frequency at which a track still carries real sound. A file header can say 48 kHz while the audio inside stops at 8 kHz.\n\nWe read the frequency spectrum of a track’s speech in one pass, up to 262,144 samples, or 5.5 seconds at 48 kHz. We smooth it over 100 Hz either side, then take the highest frequency within 60 dB of the loudest.\n\nTelephone audio cliffs near 4 kHz. A Bluetooth headset in hands-free mode caps near 8 kHz. It ships as `effective_bandwidth_hz`.",
            "p5": 6.9,
            "p50": 11.6,
            "p95": 22.3,
            "unit": "kHz"
          },
          {
            "binMax": 4.17,
            "binMin": 3.35,
